 \subsection*{Part D. PH effect on enzyme activity}
	\begin{steps}
        \newstep[] In the following you will measure enzyme activity at different PH.
     \newstep[] Find three new jumbo test tubes with stoppers and label them as A3, A4, and A5. With a clean Morh's pipette, pipette 2mL of stock 2\% starch solution into test tubes A3, A4, and A5. Make sure your Morh's pipette is clean. 
    \newstep[] Add 5 drops of 2M \ce{HCl} to test tube A3. Close the test tube with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the test tube 3 times at constant speed. Measure its PH using PH paper and write down the value in the results section. Make sure the PH is at least one PH unit different than the reference PH. If its not, add more acid until you achieve this difference. Write down this PH in the results section.
        \newstep[] Add 10 drops of 2M \ce{HCl} to test tube A4. Close the test tube with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the test tube 3 times at constant speed. Measure its PH using PH paper and write down the value in the results section. Make sure the PH is at least one PH unit different than the reference PH. If its not, add more acid until you achieve this difference. Write down this PH in the results section.
        \newstep[] Add 5 drops of 2M \ce{NaOH} to test tube A5. Close the test tube with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the test tube 3 times at constant speed. Measure its PH using PH paper and write down the value in the results section. Make sure the PH is at least one PH unit different than the reference PH. If its not, add more base until you achieve this difference. Write down this PH in the results section.
        
             \newstep[] Pipette 2mL of the enzyme solution into test tube A3 and start counting time. Mix well the content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Very quickly, using the plastic dropper, fill a cuvette 3/4 full with the mixture and start measuring the absorbance.
         \newstep[] Measure absorbance every 15 seconds for two minutes. Write down the results in the results section.
         \newstep[] Repeat the previous two steps for test tube A4 and A5.
         \newstep[] Graph a volcano plot plotting initial velocity $v_0$ (vertical axis) vs. PH (horizontal axis).
         \end{steps}


 
      
      
   
\section*{Calculations}
\mycircled{0} This is lab temperature. You will use this value for Part B as well.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{1} This is the volume of iodine added in each experiment.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{2} This is the volume of thiosulfate added in each experiment.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{3} This is the volume of peroxydisulfate added in each experiment.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{4} This is the total volume $v_{total}$ of the reacting mixture.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{5} This is the peroxydisulfate concentration in the mixture:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
    \[[\ce{S2O8^{-2}}]= \frac{v_{\ce{S2O8^{-2}}}\cdot c_{\ce{S2O8^{-2}}}}{v_{total}}=\frac{\;\;\mycircled{3}\;\; \cdot 0.2}{\;\;\mycircled{4}\;\;} \]
\mycircled{6} This is the iodine concentration in the mixture:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
    \[[\ce{I^{-}}]= \frac{v_{\ce{I^{-}}}\cdot c_{\ce{I^{-}}}}{v_{total}}=\frac{\;\;\mycircled{1}\;\; \cdot 0.2}{\;\;\mycircled{4}\;\;} \]
\mycircled{7} This is the change in thiosulfate concentration during the reaction:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
    \[\Delta [\ce{S2O3^{-2}}]= \frac{v_{\ce{S2O3^{-2}}}\cdot c_{\ce{S2O3^{-2}}}}{v_{total}}=\frac{\;\;\mycircled{2}\;\; \cdot 0.01}{\;\;\mycircled{4}\;\;} \]
\mycircled{8} This is the average time $\Delta t$ for each experiment.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{9} This is the reaction rate:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
    \[ r=\frac{\Delta [\ce{S2O3^{-2}}]}{2\cdot\Delta t}=\frac{\;\;\mycircled{7}\;\;}{2\cdot\;\;\mycircled{8}\;\;} \]
\mycircled{10} This is the peroxydisulfate order in the rate law:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
 \[
a=\frac{	\ln \left( \frac{r_{Exp 1}	}{	r_{Exp 2}	}	 \right)}{ \ln \left( \frac{\left[ \ce{S2O8^{-2}}\right]_{Exp 1} }{	\left[\ce{S2O8^{-2}}\right]_{Exp 2}} \right) }	 = \frac{	\ln \left( \;\;\mycircled{9}\;_{Exp 1}\;\; \div \;\;\mycircled{9}\;_{Exp 2}\;\;	 \right)}{ \ln \left(  \;\;\mycircled{5}\;_{Exp 1}\;\; \div \;\;\mycircled{5}\;_{Exp 2}\;\; \right) }		 	 		
 \]
\mycircled{11} This is the iodide order in the rate law:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
 \[
b=\frac{	\ln \left( \frac{r_{Exp 1}	}{	r_{Exp 3}	}	 \right)}{ \ln \left( \frac{\left[ \ce{I^{-}}\right]_{Exp 1} }{	\left[\ce{I^{-}}\right]_{Exp 3}} \right) }	 = \frac{	\ln \left( \;\;\mycircled{9}\;_{Exp 1}\;\; \div \;\;\mycircled{9}\;_{Exp 3}\;\;	 \right)}{ \ln \left(  \;\;\mycircled{6}\;_{Exp 1}\;\; \div \;\;\mycircled{6}\;_{Exp 3}\;\; \right) }		 	 		
 \]
 \mycircled{12} This is the rate constant calculated for each experiment:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
 \[
k=\frac{r}{\left[\ce{S2O8^{-2}}\right]\cdot \left[\ce{I^{-}}\right]}=\frac{\;\;\mycircled{9}\;\;}{\;\;\mycircled{5}\;\; \cdot \;\;\mycircled{6}\;\;}		 	 
\]
\mycircled{13} This is the average rate constant.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{14} The data on this row should be the same as in the first row in the Results table of Part B.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{15} This is the activation energy. You need to use the two different reaction constants at different temperatures. R is 3.184 J/K$\cdot$mol:\vspace{0.5cm}\\
 \[
E_a=\frac{T_1\cdot T_2}{T_2 - T_1}\cdot R \cdot \ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1}  	 \right) 
